@page "/knowledge-base"

<PageTitle>Metadata — Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="Metadata"
           Summary="This repository keeps its own documentation in five folders — `.arc42`, `.domain`, `.backlog`, `.tech` and `.design` — and every addressable heading in them carries a fenced `meta` block. `MetadataView` and `MetadataBadge` read that record and draw it, and each is an addition to a component that was already there. Three subpages go below the record: State, Fields — every kind of metadata on its own, drawn by the smallest component for its value — and Chapter and file, where the two shapes a whole record takes are drawn side by side. What an address in `related`, `depends-on` or `implements` actually is has its own page under Markdown → References, because a reference turns up in the prose of a chapter as well as in its record. Where the record sits and what it may say are the rules linked above."
           Guidelines="@Rules">
    <Alert Message="@OptInNotice" Role="note" CssClass="sb-callout" />

    <Story Name="A chapter is a heading, a record, and prose"
           Description="The convention, on a sample that is not invented: it is the top of this repository's own `.tech/shared.md`, pasted. A `#` heading opens the file and carries the file-level block; every `##` under it is an addressable chapter and carries its own. The fence language is `meta`, the block sits directly under its heading before any prose, and only `status` is required — every other field is written when it has a value, so an absent field reads as “not stated” and never as “stated as empty”.

Because a block sits under its heading, that is where it is drawn: the pair folds into one record and the status takes the right edge of the heading's line. That is the whole story for a `##` chapter, and every chapter that carries a block shows one.

The file-level block is the exception, and it is showing here only because this is `MarkdownView` on its own — the view has no header to put it in, so it draws it against the title. A reading surface that does have one takes it up there instead: see *File view* → **The file's own block, in the header**.

The status is a control here because this sample is read as `.tech`, and a folder names the values its status may take. Nothing on this page persists anything; a real host adds `OnStatusChanged`."
           Code="@ConventionCode"
           Source="@SharedTechSample">
        <MarkdownStory Source="@SharedTechSample"
                       RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                       KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech"
                       TestId="knowledge-base-document" />
    </Story>

    <Story Name="One block, every field, drawn"
           Description="Every shape the record draws, on one screen — and the Fields subpage takes them one at a time, with the component that draws each. `related`, `depends-on` and `implements` hold addresses, so they are parsed into references a host can follow; `alternatives` holds plain strings and never becomes a link, whatever a host wires up. `aliases` sits above the baseline of what it qualifies and may be followed where a host can place the term. `kind`, `version` and `effort` draw no visible label, because a classification chip, a `v10.0` and a points badge each say which field they came from — the name stays in the markup for a screen reader. `feature-flag` holds application keys drawn in the feature-badge family: keys, not addresses, so they never become links either.

A field the schema has never heard of — `owner` here — is kept under Extra and drawn under its own name rather than dropped, and the same collection catches a `related` entry that will not parse. A typo is only useful once it is visible.

No file writes a block like this one; it is assembled so every shape is on screen at once. A block that states nothing draws nothing: `MetadataView` renders no element at all, and `related: []` or `issue: null` read back as absent."
           Code="@RecordCode">
        <div class="sb-columns" data-testid="knowledge-base-record">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The fence body</p>
                <CodeView Source="@EveryFieldBlock" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-record-source" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">MetadataView over the record it parsed to</p>
                <MetadataView Metadata="@EveryFieldMetadata"
                                   Vocabulary="@KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(KnowledgeFolder.Tech)"
                                   AriaLabel="Chapter metadata"
                                   TestId="knowledge-base-record-view" />
                <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-base-record-plain">@PlainStringSummary</p>
                <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-base-record-extra">@ReferenceAndExtraSummary</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The rows are a component, so the two halves can be drawn apart"
           Description="Everything the record states except its status is `MetadataFields` — the `dl` and the rows in it. A component of its own because the two halves of a record are not always drawn in the same place.

In a document they are: the status closes the heading it describes and the rows hang under it, which is what `MetadataView` composes above. The file pane cannot do that. Its header is two lines by contract, and the rows are one row per stated field — so a file stating `related` and `depends-on` gave that header four lines, and nothing in the header could truncate its way out, because the rows are the content rather than an overflow of it. So `FileHeader` draws the status in the header and hands these to a strip beneath it, the way it already treats a file&#39;s frontmatter.

Nothing here knows which of the two it is in. The order of the rows and the question each one asks about emptiness are `MetadataView`&#39;s, moved rather than rewritten, so a record renders the same rows in either host — and `HasAny` is asked by the host before it commits to the element that would wrap them, because an empty `dl` is a gap with a gap&#39;s spacing around it."
           Code="@FieldsCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-base-fields">
            <p class="sb-label">MetadataFields over the same record, with no status and no wrapper</p>
            <MetadataFields Metadata="@EveryFieldMetadata" TestId="knowledge-base-fields-view" />
            <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-base-fields-state">@FieldsSummary</p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="A field is a row, and the row is a component"
           Description="Every field above is the same three elements — a row, the field&#39;s name, and a cell holding whatever its values draw as — and only the cell differs. `MetadataField` is those three elements, and the record renders one per field it has something to say about.

It was five near-identical fragments inside `MetadataView` before that, which meant the `dt`/`dd` pairing a screen reader depends on was rebuilt once per field and had five chances to be got wrong instead of one. What a host reuses it for is a record of its own: the row takes a `BaseClass`, a label class and a value class, so a settings panel or a spec sheet can lay out label-and-value pairs without a second implementation of the pair.

It never asks whether there is anything to say. That question belongs to the caller, because only the caller knows what empty means for its own field — an absent list, a blank scalar and an estimate of zero are three different answers, and zero is an estimate that still shows. The `dl` belongs to the record too: a row that brought its own list would break the pairing for every other row.

The last row draws no visible name. Some values say which field they came from without help, and in front of one of those the label is a dimmed word restating what the value already said — so `ShowLabel` hides it. What it does not do is remove it: the `dt` stays in the list wearing `sr-only`, because a value read out with no field name is worse to hear than a name nobody needed to see. The row takes a `--bare` modifier off whatever `BaseClass` it was given, which is what lets the stylesheet give the value the column the hidden label is no longer holding.

The bottom pair is the same two rows pointed at names that are not the library&#39;s, which is the whole of what adopting it costs."
           Code="@FieldCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-base-field-rows">
            <dl class="knowledge-fields">
                <MetadataField Label="depends-on">
                    <KnowledgeReferenceLink Reference="@FieldReference" />
                </MetadataField>
                <MetadataField Label="kind">
                    <code class="knowledge-value">framework</code>
                </MetadataField>
                <MetadataField Label="effort">
                    <Badge Kind="effort">5 pts</Badge>
                </MetadataField>
                <MetadataField Label="feature-flag" TestId="knowledge-base-field-flag">
                    <Badge Kind="feature">inbox-pane</Badge>
                </MetadataField>
                <MetadataField Label="version" ShowLabel="false" TestId="knowledge-base-field-bare">
                    <code class="knowledge-value">v10.0</code>
                </MetadataField>
            </dl>
            <p class="sb-label">The same rows under a host&#39;s own names</p>
            <dl class="sb-stack">
                <MetadataField Label="kind"
                                    BaseClass="sb-stack"
                                    LabelCssClass="sb-label"
                                    ValueCssClass="sb-state">framework</MetadataField>
                <MetadataField Label="version"
                                    BaseClass="sb-stack"
                                    LabelCssClass="sb-label"
                                    ValueCssClass="sb-state">10.0</MetadataField>
            </dl>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Every field, and which folder writes it"
           Description="The schema is small, closed and mostly folder-specific: `kind` and `version` describe a technology, `implements` is how a backlog item says what it delivers, `aliases` belongs to the one file per context that names terms.

The middle column is the one that catches people. Three fields hold references — addresses that parse into a path and an optional chapter — and three hold plain strings that are never parsed. Every row carries the line as a file actually writes it: a scalar takes its value bare, and both list kinds take a list.">
        <dl class="sb-grid" data-testid="knowledge-base-fields">
            @foreach (var field in Fields)
            {
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <dt class="sb-label">@field.Name</dt>
                    <dd class="sb-stack">
                        <p class="sb-state">@field.Shape — @field.Folders</p>
                        <CodeView Source="@field.Line" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code"
                                  TestId="@($"knowledge-base-field-{field.Name}-source")" />
                        <p>@field.Meaning</p>
                    </dd>
                </div>
            }
        </dl>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Nothing renders differently until a host asks"
           Description="The same document twice, with its source above both. On the left `MarkdownView` has been given nothing new and the `meta` fence is a fenced code block; on the right `RenderKnowledgeMetadata` and `KnowledgeFolder` are set, and the fence becomes the record it was written as, folded into the heading above it.

The default is off, which is the whole compatibility story: most text in this product is an entry body somebody typed, and there a fence that happens to say `meta` is a listing like any other.

The seam is one test in front of branches that already existed. An `MdCode` block has three answers where it had two — a metadata record, a diagram where the language names one, and otherwise the code block it always was — and the metadata test runs first, gated on the opt-in."
           Code="@OptInCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-base-opt-in">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The document both columns render</p>
                <CodeView Source="@ChapterSample.Trim()" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-opt-in-source" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-columns">
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <p class="sb-label">Default — the fence is a fence</p>
                    <div data-testid="knowledge-base-opt-in-off">
                        <MarkdownView Blocks="@ChapterBlocks" />
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="sb-stack">
                    <p class="sb-label">Opted in — the fence is a record</p>
                    <div data-testid="knowledge-base-opt-in-on">
                        <MarkdownView Blocks="@ChapterBlocks"
                                      RenderKnowledgeMetadata="true"
                                      KnowledgeFolder="KnowledgeFolder.Tech" />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="The strip that was already there did not move"
           Description="The two badges below do not look alike, and that is deliberate. The upper one is the legacy two-parameter form and keeps the markup it had when it was written: one status span carrying `knowledge-status--ready`, one `code.knowledge-related` per relation, no description list, no link and no control. The lower one is the typed form.

The two are branches kept apart rather than layered, so asking for nothing new gets the old markup and asking for a block, a folder, a resolver or a callback gets the new one. `MetadataBadgeCompatibilityTests` asserts the legacy markup attribute by attribute.

Both are fed from the same block above. The typed status is a control where the legacy one is a word, because naming the folder names the values it may take, so the record offers a `StatusSelector` — drawn as the same badge a read-only status is. `OnStatusChanged` carries the choice out; the line under it reports the last status this page was told about."
           Code="@CompatibilityCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-base-compatibility">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The block both badges were read from</p>
                <CodeView Source="@RoadmapBlock" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-compatibility-source" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">Two parameters — frozen markup, the old capsule, deliberately not updated</p>
                <MetadataBadge Status="@RoadmapMetadata.Status"
                               Related="@LegacyRelated"
                               AriaLabel="Entry metadata"
                               TestId="knowledge-base-legacy-badge" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The same fields, typed — deliberately the application status badge instead, folder-aware and listened to</p>
                <MetadataBadge Metadata="@RoadmapMetadata"
                               Folder="KnowledgeFolder.Backlog"
                               OnStatusChanged="HearStatus"
                               AriaLabel="Entry metadata"
                               TestId="knowledge-base-typed-badge" />
                <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-base-status-heard">@StatusHeard</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Handing your own heading in"
           Description="The status shares the heading's line only if the two share a parent, so `HeadingContent` is where a host hands its heading in rather than placing it above. The heading below is the markup `MarkdownView` emits for one, which is what the record's stylesheet expects to sit beside; a host handing in its own element brings that element's margins with it.

Two widths, one sample. At full width the heading fits; at a navigation pane's width it wraps, and what to look for is that the status does not move — it stays on the first line at the right edge. Three things do that together: the line does not wrap, the heading may shrink below its own longest word so it is the item that gives, and the row aligns on first baselines."
           Code="@ComponentCode">
        <div class="sb-stack" data-testid="knowledge-base-heading">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The chapter as it is written on disk</p>
                <CodeView Source="@HowToSource" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-heading-source" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">Full width — the heading fits</p>
                <MetadataView Metadata="@TechMetadata"
                                   Vocabulary="@KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(KnowledgeFolder.Tech)"
                                   CssClass="sb-metadata-panel"
                                   AriaLabel="Chapter metadata"
                                   TestId="knowledge-base-heading-wide">
                    <Heading>
                        <p class="md-heading md-heading--2" role="heading" aria-level="2">@ChapterHeading</p>
                    </Heading>
                </MetadataView>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack sb-narrow">
                <p class="sb-label">The same record at 28rem — the heading wraps, the status does not move</p>
                <MetadataView Metadata="@TechMetadata"
                                   Vocabulary="@KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(KnowledgeFolder.Tech)"
                                   CssClass="sb-metadata-panel"
                                   AriaLabel="Chapter metadata"
                                   TestId="knowledge-base-heading-narrow">
                    <Heading>
                        <p class="md-heading md-heading--2" role="heading" aria-level="2">@LongChapterHeading</p>
                    </Heading>
                </MetadataView>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The stylesheet rule that holds the line together</p>
                <CodeView Source="@WrapCode" Language="razor" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-heading-css" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Parsing a fence"
           Description="`Parse` takes the text inside the fence — the caller has already stripped the fence lines — and hands back a `MetadataRecord`. `ParseFence` is the same call with the language check in front of it, so a block that was never metadata comes back `Empty` rather than half-read. `IsMetaBlock` is exposed separately for the case where the answer decides what you render rather than what you parse, which is how `MarkdownView` uses it.

It is hand-rolled rather than handed to a YAML library: this library carries no dependency beyond the Blazor packages, and the block is a flat list of scalars with lists one level deep. It is forgiving — `related: []`, `issue: null`, and inline lists both quoted and bare all read back the same way.

Parse once per block and keep the result; `MarkdownView` holds a small dictionary keyed by block for exactly that. The source beside this story is the whole fence, opening line included, because that line is what `ParseFence` is given."
           Code="@ParseFenceCode">
        <div class="sb-columns" data-testid="knowledge-base-fence">
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">The fence, language and all</p>
                <CodeView Source="@FencedTechBlock" Language="markdown" AllowCopy="false" CssClass="sb-raw__code" TestId="knowledge-base-fence-source" />
            </div>
            <div class="sb-stack">
                <p class="sb-label">ParseFence over that language and that body</p>
                <MetadataView Metadata="@MetadataReader.ParseFence(MetadataReader.FenceLanguage, TechBlock)"
                                   TestId="knowledge-base-fence-view" />
                <p class="sb-state" data-testid="knowledge-base-fence-state">@FenceSummary</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DesignGuideline[] Rules =
    [
        new("content-editing.md#knowledge-metadata-blocks", "Which block is drawn where — the file's in a header, a chapter's beside its heading."),
        new("README.md#status-vocabulary", "The status words a knowledge chapter may carry."),
        new("color-scheme.md#badge-and-chip-tones", "The tone each status and reference kind is drawn in."),
        new("content-editing.md#structured-metadata-sigils", "The fenced metadata grammar this block reads."),
        new("typography-and-layout.md#metadata-lines", "How a record of fields sits above the prose it describes.")
    ];

    private const string OptInNotice =
        "Everything on this page and its two subpages is opt-in. MarkdownView renders a meta fence as a "
        + "fenced code block until a host sets RenderKnowledgeMetadata, and MetadataBadge renders the "
        + "strip it always did until something asks for more — so a surface that adopts none of this is "
        + "unaffected by all of it.";

    /// <summary>One field of the schema, with the line a file writes it as. The
    /// line is not decoration: the shape column claims a syntax, and the line is
    /// what shows it.</summary>
    private sealed record FieldRow(string Name, string Shape, string Folders, string Line, string Meaning);

    /// <summary>The last status this page was told about, which is what
    /// OnStatusChanged buys a host over letting the control keep its own.</summary>
    private string? _heardStatus;

    /// <summary>The top of this repository's own .tech/shared.md, verbatim.</summary>
    private const string SharedTechSample = """
        # Shared Technologies

        ```meta
        status: candidate
        related: [".tech/technology-graph.md", ".arc42/02-constraints.md#technical-constraints"]
        ```

        > Technologies used by more than one channel. Every layer file points at these
        > chapters with `depends-on` instead of redefining them locally.

        ## Markdown

        ```meta
        status: adopted
        kind: format
        related: [".arc42/02-constraints.md#technical-constraints", ".arc42/08-crosscutting-concepts.md#storage-and-sync"]
        ```

        The canonical storage format for all user content: inbox items, backlog items,
        knowledge notes, and prompts.
        """;

    /// <summary>One chapter of .tech/shared.md, used for the on and off pair.</summary>
    private const string ChapterSample = """
        ## .NET Runtime

        ```meta
        status: candidate
        kind: runtime
        version: "10.0"
        alternatives: ["Node.js only", "Rust + Tauri"]
        ```

        The primary managed runtime for the desktop, mobile, IDE and cloud channels.
        """;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> ChapterBlocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(ChapterSample);

    /// <summary>Every field at once, which is the one block on these pages that no
    /// folder would actually write: the schema is folder-specific and half of
    /// these fields are meaningless together. Values are taken from the real
    /// blocks they belong to — the .tech/cloud.md chapter, the Backlog Entry term
    /// in .domain/backlog/naming.md — rather than invented, so every line here is
    /// a line this repository contains somewhere.</summary>
    private const string EveryFieldBlock = """
        status: adopted
        kind: framework
        version: "10.0"
        effort: 5
        issue: JSdotNet/Backlog#118
        depends-on: [".tech/shared.md#net-runtime", ".tech/shared.md#c-language"]
        related: [".arc42/04-solution-strategy.md#thin-cloud-rich-desktop"]
        implements: [.domain/backlog/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning]
        feature-flag: [inbox-pane, inbox-filters]
        aliases: [TaskItem, backlog_entry_id, backlog_item_id]
        alternatives: ["Azure Functions", "Controller-based ASP.NET Core"]
        owner: platform-team
        """;

    private static readonly MetadataRecord EveryFieldMetadata = MetadataReader.Parse(EveryFieldBlock);

    /// <summary>The heading the .tech block below sits under, so the heading story
    /// can show what a host gets for handing one in.</summary>
    private const string ChapterHeading = "ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs";

    /// <summary>A heading of the length a real chapter reaches — this one is the
    /// shape of an arc42 section title — so the narrow render is reviewed against
    /// something a file would actually contain rather than filler.</summary>
    private const string LongChapterHeading =
        "ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs, and the routing conventions the sync service is expected to follow";

    /// <summary>The ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs chapter of this repository's .tech/cloud.md.</summary>
    private const string TechBlock = """
        status: candidate
        kind: framework
        depends-on: [".tech/shared.md#net-runtime", ".tech/shared.md#c-language"]
        related: [".arc42/04-solution-strategy.md#thin-cloud-rich-desktop"]
        alternatives: ["Azure Functions", "Controller-based ASP.NET Core"]
        """;

    private static readonly MetadataRecord TechMetadata = MetadataReader.Parse(TechBlock);

    /// <summary>The block both badges below are given, shown as the source of
    /// each. A backlog entry's own metadata, with an address under two different
    /// relations.</summary>
    private const string RoadmapBlock = """
        status: ready
        implements: [.domain/backlog/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning]
        related: [.domain/environment/features.md#feature-environment-aware-work-context]
        """;

    private static readonly MetadataRecord RoadmapMetadata = MetadataReader.Parse(RoadmapBlock);

    /// <summary>What a caller passes today: the addresses as the flat list of
    /// strings the two-parameter form takes, with the relation each was written
    /// under lost on the way in — that flattening is half of what this story is
    /// contrasting. Read off the same block rather than restated beside it, so the
    /// two badges are provably rendering one source.</summary>
    private static readonly string[] LegacyRelated =
        [.. RoadmapMetadata.References.Select(reference => reference.Raw)];

    /// <summary>The chapter as it is written on disk: the heading, then the block
    /// directly under it. Built from the same two constants the example renders,
    /// so the source cannot describe a chapter the view is not showing.</summary>
    private static string HowToSource => $"## {ChapterHeading}\n\n```{MetadataReader.FenceLanguage}\n{TechBlock}\n```";

    /// <summary>The same block with its fence lines. The fence story is about the
    /// language on the opening fence as much as the body inside it, so the body
    /// alone would be missing the argument.</summary>
    private static string FencedTechBlock => $"```{MetadataReader.FenceLanguage}\n{TechBlock}\n```";

    /// <summary>What the language check answers, and what a non-meta fence parses to.</summary>
    private static string FenceSummary =>
        $"IsMetaBlock(\"meta\") is {MetadataReader.IsMetaBlock("meta")}; "
        + $"IsMetaBlock(\"mermaid\") is {MetadataReader.IsMetaBlock("mermaid")}; "
        + $"the same body through ParseFence(\"mermaid\", …) comes back empty: "
        + $"{MetadataReader.ParseFence("mermaid", TechBlock).IsEmpty}.";

    /// <summary>What came back under the three plain-string fields. Counted off
    /// the parsed record rather than off the block, so the claim is the
    /// component's answer and not a restatement of the source above it.</summary>
    private static string PlainStringSummary =>
        $"{EveryFieldMetadata.Aliases.Count} aliases and "
        + $"{EveryFieldMetadata.Alternatives.Count} strings under alternatives — none parsed, and none a "
        + "link unless a host can place the term an alias names. "
        + $"{EveryFieldMetadata.FeatureFlag.Count} feature flags, which are keys rather than words and "
        + "are never followable at all.";

    private static string ReferenceAndExtraSummary =>
        $"{EveryFieldMetadata.References.Count} parsed references across related, depends-on and "
        + $"implements. Kept under Extra: {string.Join(", ", EveryFieldMetadata.Extra.Keys)}.";

    private string StatusHeard => _heardStatus is null
        ? "Nothing heard yet. Change the status on the badge above."
        : $"OnStatusChanged last reported: {_heardStatus}.";

    private void HearStatus(string status) => _heardStatus = status;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<FieldRow> Fields =
    [
        new("status", "scalar", "every folder",
            "status: adopted",
            "The only required field, and the only one every folder writes. What it is allowed to say "
            + "depends on the folder, which is the State subpage."),

        new("related", "references", "every folder",
            "related: [\".arc42/02-constraints.md#technical-constraints\"]",
            "What this chapter points at for context, with no claim about order. The general-purpose "
            + "relation, and the one to reach for when the others do not fit."),

        new("depends-on", "references", ".domain features, .backlog, .tech",
            "depends-on: [\".tech/shared.md#net-runtime\"]",
            "What has to land first. Says something stronger than related does, which is why a "
            + "technology chapter uses it to point at the shared runtime it sits on."),

        new("implements", "references", ".backlog",
            "implements: [.domain/backlog/features.md#feature-roadmap-planning]",
            "What a backlog item delivers, addressed into .domain. This is the field that ties a work "
            + "item to the feature it exists for."),

        new("issue", "scalar", ".backlog, .arc42 decisions",
            "issue: JSdotNet/Backlog#118",
            "The tracking issue: a URL, or the owner/repo#number shorthand. Stored exactly as authored "
            + "— only the URL form can be followed from here, because the shorthand needs a remote "
            + "this library knows nothing about."),

        new("feature-flag", "application keys", ".domain features",
            "feature-flag: [inbox-pane, inbox-filters]",
            "The key or keys of the application feature flag that delivers this chapter in the running "
            + "product. Plain application identifiers and deliberately not references: the flag lives "
            + "in the application's own catalog, so there is no chapter to address and no graph edge. "
            + "An identity link and not a status mapping — how settled the written model is and "
            + "whether the running behaviour can be relied on are different questions."),

        new("aliases", "qualifiers", ".domain naming",
